 ## How each cue was handled
 
-- **Delivery note.** A cue inside a person-chat line was removed and became a `#delivery:` tag at the end of the line (syntax and limits from `TtsService::DELIVERY_TAG` on `build/voice-direction-tag`: one note, under 200 characters, last one wins). The note is written for the voice: pace, volume, mood. Pure movement cues were turned into a note only where they shape the delivery ("pointing something out", "not looking up").
-- **Narrator line.** Three cues were physical beats the scene would lose: Sister Doyle looking round for the first time (the README's own example of a scene turn), Irina holding out her wrists, and Kemal pocketing his watch before the fight. Each is now a short third person, present tense narrator line just above the spoken line, written the way that file already writes narration (inline `Narrator:` in the ward nurse file; `#speaker:narrator`, then the NPC's `#speaker:` restored, in the Irina and minder files). Each narrator line sits above the knot's or choice's tags and assignments, so no tag moved to a different line.
-- **Phone.** Ghost's lines in `m02_phone_ghost.ink` are a voiced video call (`npcType: phone`), which sends no delivery note, so the cue was removed and nothing added.
+- **Delivery note.** A cue inside a person-chat line was removed and became a `#delivery:` tag at the end of the line (syntax from `TtsService::DELIVERY_TAG` on `build/voice-direction-tag`: one note per line, last one wins; the 200-character cap is `MAX_DELIVERY_LENGTH`, checked in the TTS endpoint, and `clean_delivery` only collapses whitespace). The note is written for the voice: pace, volume, mood. A movement cue became a note only as the delivery it causes ("not looking up" became "flat, uninterested"); no note describes movement alone.
+- **Narrator line.** Five cues were physical beats the scene would lose: Sister Doyle looking round for the first time (the README's own example of a scene turn), Irina holding out her wrists, Kemal pocketing his watch before the fight, and (added in review round 1) Bernie putting the handset back to her ear, so her next line reads as said into the phone, and Reeves tilting his head at the wall phone, the only pointer for "I rang control from that phone". Each is now a short third person, present tense narrator line just above the spoken line, written the way that file already writes narration (inline `Narrator:` in the ward nurse, receptionist and asset files; `#speaker:narrator`, then the NPC's `#speaker:` restored, in the Irina and minder files). Each narrator line sits above the knot's or choice's tags and assignments, so no tag moved to a different line.
+- **Text chats left alone.** Ghost's terminal chat in `m02_phone_ghost.ink` is read, not heard: its cued lines sit in `start`, `ghost_philosophy`, `on_cracker_taken`, `ransom_paid_response` and `ghost_final_statement`, outside the knots the two voiced video calls reach (`on_recovery_console`, `on_console_again`). On-screen beats such as "*something shifts*" stay in text chats, so the file is as it was at `1f959061d`. No other phone ink in m02 to m07 had a cue.
 
-Nothing else changed: same words, same line order, no logic, choices, diverts or other tags touched. `tagdiff.mjs --ref 1f959061d` over the six missions shows only the added `#delivery:` tags and the two `#speaker:narrator` tags.
+Nothing else changed: same words, same line order, no logic, choices, diverts or other tags touched. `tagdiff.mjs --ref 1f959061d` over the six missions shows only the added `#delivery:` tags and the two `#speaker:narrator` tags (each followed by the NPC's `#speaker:` restored); the m02 narrator lines use the inline `Narrator:` prefix and add no tag.
 
